<!-- language -->|official=[[Baliscano language|Baliscano]]|spoken=See [[Languages of Balisca]]}}

Balisca had an official resident population of 214 million in 2025, according to the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS). Balisca is the [[List of countries and dependencies by population|eight-most populous country]] in the world and the most populous in the [[Northern Hemisphere|Northern]] and [[Western Hemisphere|Western Hemispheres]]. [[Baliscans]] are mainly concentrated in coastal areas, with the vast majority (71.64'''%''') of the Baliscan population living with {{Convert|50|km|mi}} of a coastline.

As of the 2025 census, the majority of Balisca population identifies as [[Mestizo Baliscans|''Mestizo'']] (64.8%), commonly translated as "mixed" in [[Mercian language|Mercian]]. [[Kafto-Baliscans]] constitute the single largest pan-ethnic group at 27.71%, followed by [[Illypnian Baliscans]] (23.10%), [[Bayani Baliscans|Levantines]] (24.16%), [[Fosian Baliscans]] (15.83%) and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous peoples]] (9.2%). Immigration patterns reflect these demographics, with most new migrants historically arriving from [[Kafto-Illypnifosia]], though immigration from the [[Algarves]] has increased in recent decades. By several metrics, including ethnic background, religious affiliation, and percentage of rural and urban divide, the region of [[Carpinchera]] is the most representative of the larger demography of Balisca.

Balisca has been noted for its relatively high levels of [[W:Miscegenation|ethnic intermarriage]], a trend historically met with social acceptance. A 2022 study found that Baliscans have an average of 35% Indigenous ancestry, though the concept of being "mixed" is not commonly used within Indigenous communities, where identity is often based on cultural affiliation rather than ancestry alone.

Balisca is a {{W|multilingualism|multilingual}} state, and multilingualism is widely encouraged at the communal and cantonal levels. [[Baliscano]]—the world's most widely spoken [[W:Creole language|creole language]]—has effectively been the official language of the entire country since 1883. Besides Baliscano, other territorialized languages include the indigenous [[Kurupiran-Nyengatú languages]], [[W:Aragonese language|Jacano]], [[W:Sartan language|Sartan]], [[Bizkarran language|Bizkarran]], {{W|Judaeo-Spanish language|Ladino}} (Judaeo-Sartan), [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Mozbayani]] and [[Beron language|Beron]]. As of 2022, Baliscano is estimated to be spoken proficiently by 84%, Mercian by 30%, Mozbayani by 16% and Kurupirangüe by 4% of the Baliscan population. Some of the most spoken foreign languages used by the immigrant communities include {{W|Moroccan Arabic|Tamazghan Bayani}}, [[W:Chinese language|Jade]] (all varieties), [[Imo language|Imo]] and [[Kemali language|Kemali]].

